node_modules/
# .env and any hand-rolled backup of it carry a live API_KEY. This repo is
# public, so an untracked .env* in the working tree is one `git add -A` away
# from publishing a credential. Ignore the whole class rather than one spelling
# (.env.bak, .env.old, .env.backup, .env.2 are all just as likely as
# .env.bak-<date>), then re-admit the tracked template.
.env*
!.env.example
# Sparkle writes .sparkle/merge-policy.json into every agent worktree it cuts.
# Nothing in this repo reads it. Ignoring it in-tree is what makes the exclusion
# portable: a machine that lacks a local .sparkle/ exclude sees the marker as an
# untracked change, and a permanently dirty worktree is enough for teardown to
# refuse ("holds uncommitted changes") and strand it. Ignore the directory rather
# than the one file: the marker set is the app's to change, not this repo's.
.sparkle/
.machine_config_hash
.reporting-state.json
.agentsview-update-check
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
